---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
const base=import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/,'');
---
<Layout title="Codex monitor"><div class="wrap"><header class="page-head"><p class="eyebrow">A desk companion built from small diagnostics</p><h1>Your agents.<br />At a glance.</h1><p class="lede">A landscape dashboard for Codex usage, agent activity and voice messages, connected to a bridge on your own computer.</p></header><div class="article-grid"><article class="prose"><figure><img class="screen" src={`${base}/docs/images/codex-monitor-screen.png`} width="320" height="240" alt="Actual monitor display showing live usage and a working agent" /><figcaption>Actual FNK0104B LCD readback, 4 October 2026. This is a captured moment, not a live dashboard.</figcaption></figure>
<h2>What you can do</h2><ul><li>See remaining five-hour and weekly usage with reset countdowns.</li><li>See animated agent activity and select an agent for a message.</li><li>Say “Hi ESP” for the five local commands shown in the listening guide.</li><li>Record a message by touch or voice, then send it through your bridge to a configured transcription service.</li><li>Adjust alert volume, idle screen timeout and USB Micro layout through Web Bluetooth.</li><li>Configure Wi-Fi with the board's on-screen setup code.</li></ul><p>Approvals stay in the Codex client. Visibility of threads running in a separate Codex process depends on what the bridge's app-server connection can observe.</p>
<h2>USB Micro controls</h2><p>With Desktop connected over USB HID, the default layout shows three stable agent slots and four joystick buttons: Up, Down, Right and Left. Choose six slots in setup if needed. Tap the Micro indicator for the other Desktop keys. Customize joystick actions in Desktop; direction mapping still needs verification on the host. BLE stays available for browser settings as FNK0104B-MONITOR.</p><h2>The connection, explained</h2><div class="card"><p><strong>Board → Wi-Fi → local Python bridge → signed-in Codex app-server</strong></p><p>Recorded message → bridge → configured transcription service → selected Codex thread.</p><p>Browser → Bluetooth LE → board preferences or Wi-Fi provisioning.</p></div><p>GitHub Pages serves this guide and firmware files. Your computer runs the bridge. The browser is not the status server, and USB is not needed for normal Wi-Fi status after setup.</p>
<h2>Get your own monitor working</h2><ol><li><strong>Prepare the network.</strong> Use 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi that can reach your computer. A monitor with no credentials opens secure provisioning; use <a href={`${base}/setup.html#setup`}>Device setup</a> with its displayed code.</li><li><strong>Configure the private bridge.</strong> Follow the repository setup walkthrough to put your computer's LAN address and a generated shared key in the ignored local header. The public browser image has neither.</li><li><strong>Run the bridge.</strong> Use the signed-in Codex app-server in the same environment. WSL may require LAN forwarding; a Windows Codex socket does not automatically exist inside WSL.</li><li><strong>Build and upload locally.</strong> Use the named <code>codex-monitor</code> PlatformIO environment, including its speech models. Inspect serial at 115200 and check Wi-Fi and application status separately.</li><li><strong>Configure preferences.</strong> Connect to <code>FNK0104B-MONITOR</code> in the browser picker for volume and idle timeout.</li><li><strong>Add transcription if wanted.</strong> Configure a compatible service behind the bridge. Local screen/status commands already work without it.</li></ol><p><a class="button" href="https://github.com/cmwen/FNK0104B/blob/main/docs/monitor-setup.md">Open the full setup walkthrough ↗</a></p>
<h2>Listening is two interactions</h2><p>“Hi ESP” opens a twelve-second window for “start listening”, “go back”, “show status”, “turn on the screen” and “turn off the screen”. “Show status” holds the quota view; “go back” or a card tap restores agent overview.</p><p>A message capture allows ten seconds to start speaking, approximately five seconds of silence before submission and a thirty-second maximum. Tap to stop early. Recognition pauses during capture/submission; quiet mode and attention tones are coordinated with listening.</p>
<h2>Other screen states</h2><div class="screens"><figure><img src={`${base}/docs/images/monitor-command.png`} width="320" height="240" loading="lazy" alt="Firmware-rendered local command listening guide" /><figcaption>Command listening · host preview with sample data.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${base}/docs/images/monitor-recording.png`} width="320" height="240" loading="lazy" alt="Firmware-rendered message recording panel" /><figcaption>Message recording · host preview with sample data.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${base}/docs/images/monitor-idle.png`} width="320" height="240" loading="lazy" alt="Firmware-rendered idle quota view" /><figcaption>Idle quota view · host preview with sample data.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${base}/docs/images/monitor-offline.png`} width="320" height="240" loading="lazy" alt="Firmware-rendered offline monitor state" /><figcaption>Offline feedback · host preview with sample data.</figcaption></figure></div>
<h2>What has been verified?</h2><p>The current monitor was uploaded with verified image hashes. Serial confirmed saved Wi-Fi reconnection, speech models ready, continuous audio-front-end processing and live Codex SSE. The LCD screenshot above was captured from the board. Previous mock runs exercised error/recovery and usage endpoints.</p><p>Spoken recognition accuracy, meter sensitivity, sustained memory stability, actual long capture and running-agent message interactions remain <strong>UNKNOWN</strong> until exercised. Read the dated evidence before treating a build or a host preview as a physical test.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/cmwen/FNK0104B/blob/main/docs/monitor-readiness.md">Monitor readiness and remaining checks ↗</a></p>
</article><aside class="aside"><h3>Capabilities in this project</h3>{[['display-touch','Display & touch'],['bluetooth','Web Bluetooth'],['storage','NVS & memory'],['speech','Local speech & ML'],['microphone','Recording messages'],['screen-idle','Inactivity & wake']].map(([slug,label])=><a href={`${base}/features/${slug}/`}>{label} →</a>)}<h3>Keep exploring</h3><a href={`${base}/journey/`}>How we got here →</a><a href={`${base}/firmware/codex-monitor/`}>Firmware & installation →</a></aside></div></div></Layout>
